# gcr -- certificate and key display, and the ssh-agent the session runs.
#
# THIS IS THE GTK4 GENERATION, gcr-4, and it is the one the live session links:
# gnome-shell 48 takes gcr-4 >= 3.90.0 and gnome-control-center 48 takes
# >= 4.1.0. pkgs/gcr3 is the same project's previous generation, packaged
# separately and stripped to two libraries because gnome-keyring demands them.
# Read that recipe before touching this one; the pair only coexists on purpose.
#
# Not an LFS package, so it is pinned here rather than in versions.env.
# BLFS 12.4 pins 4.4.0.1.
SRC_URL=https://download.gnome.org/sources/gcr/4.4/gcr-4.4.0.1.tar.xz
SRC_SHA256=0c3c341e49f9f4f2532a4884509804190a0c2663e6120360bb298c5d174a8098
SRC_DIR=gcr-4.4.0.1

# ==============================================================================
# THE ssh-agent COLLISION, WHICH NEITHER SIDE CAN REACH
# ==============================================================================
# gcr-3 and gcr-4 are coinstallable, and upstream did the work to make them so:
# libgck-1/libgcr-base-3 against libgck-2/libgcr-4, headers in gck-1 and gcr-3
# against gck-2 and gcr-4, gcr-viewer against gcr-viewer-gtk4, gcr-ssh-askpass
# against gcr4-ssh-askpass. EVERY name carries its version EXCEPT ONE:
#
#   gcr-3.41.2/gcr/meson.build:251   executable('gcr-ssh-agent', install_dir: libexecdir)
#   gcr-4.4.0.1/gcr/meson.build:260  executable('gcr-ssh-agent', install_dir: libexecdir)
#
# Both land on /usr/libexec/gcr-ssh-agent. Two packages owning one path is a
# hard install error with no override (README), so shipping both unmodified
# would make the package set uninstallable. Upstream's "coinstallable" claim is
# true only with the flag its own build instructions quietly pass.
#
# -Dssh_agent=false, AND THE COLLISION TURNS OUT TO BE UNREACHABLE ANYWAY.
#
# The decision was that gcr-4 should own the agent -- the opposite of BLFS,
# which disables it here and lets gcr-3 keep it, because a book resolves a
# collision in favour of ITS incumbent and the incumbency is inverted in this
# tree. That reasoning still stands and is recorded below for the day it
# becomes actionable. It is not actionable today:
#
#     meson.build:97   if get_option('ssh_agent')
#     meson.build:98     ssh_add_path = find_program('ssh-add').full_path()
#
# find_program with no required: false. OPENSSH IS NOT PACKAGED IN THIS
# DISTRIBUTION -- not in pkgs/, not in the published index -- so ssh-add does
# not exist and -Dssh_agent=true fails at configure:
#
#     ERROR: Program 'ssh-add' not found or not executable
#
# gcr-3 fails on the same program for a different reason (see pkgs/gcr3, where
# its own lookup is broken in both directions), so NEITHER generation can build
# gcr-ssh-agent here. The path both would claim is never created by either.
#
# AND THE FEATURE WOULD BE POINTLESS IF IT BUILT. gcr-ssh-agent exists to hold
# passphrases for ssh keys and to hand them to ssh; with no OpenSSH there is no
# ssh client to serve. Packaging OpenSSH to make an ssh-agent buildable, for a
# distribution that has no ssh, is the tail wagging the dog.
#
# WHAT SURVIVES IS THE DECISION AND ITS ENFORCEMENT. If OpenSSH is ever
# packaged, exactly one of this pair may set ssh_agent=true, and it is this
# one. Both install.sh files assert /usr/libexec/gcr-ssh-agent is ABSENT today,
# so the day someone flips a flag the build fails naming the path rather than
# producing two packages that install separately and cannot coexist.
#
# ==============================================================================
# THE REST
# ==============================================================================
# -Dcrypto=libgcrypt. Same combo as libsecret (libgcrypt or gnutls, defaulting
# libgcrypt) and pinned for the same reason: both backends are packaged now, so
# the default is a live choice rather than a foregone one, and libsecret, gcr
# and gnome-keyring are one keyring stack that should carry one crypto
# implementation. Asserted as DT_NEEDED in install.sh.
#
# -Dsystemd=disabled. 'auto', and it resolves off here ANYWAY -- meson.build
# 104-106 asks for libsystemd AND systemd and gates on both being found, and
# elogind provides a libsystemd.pc symlink but no systemd.pc, so the
# conjunction fails closed. Named so the outcome is stated rather than reached
# through a compatibility symlink and a two-term AND. It also keeps the
# gcr-ssh-agent.service and .socket units out of the payload.
#
# -Dgpg_path=/usr/bin/gpg. With it unset, meson.build:64 runs
# find_program('gpg2', 'gpg') -- NO required: false, so a missing gpg is fatal
# -- and bakes the resolved absolute path into GPG_EXECUTABLE. gnupg is
# packaged and installs /usr/bin/gpg and NOT gpg2, so the probe currently
# succeeds on its second candidate. Naming the path removes the dependency on
# probe order and on which of the two names a future gnupg installs.
#
# -Dvapi=false. Defaults TRUE and needs vapigen from vala, which is not
# packaged. The one that fails on a missing program rather than a library.
#
# -Dgtk_doc=false. Defaults TRUE and needs gi-docgen, not packaged.
#
# -Dintrospection=true is already the default and is kept deliberately:
# gnome-shell reaches gcr through Gcr-4 rather than by linking it.
#
# -Dgtk4=true is the default and is what makes this the gtk4 generation at all.
MESON_ARGS="-Dgtk4=true -Dcrypto=libgcrypt -Dssh_agent=false \
-Dsystemd=disabled -Dgpg_path=/usr/bin/gpg \
-Dvapi=false -Dgtk_doc=false -Dintrospection=true"
